on monday mexico supreme court  decriminalized cannabis use  today is a historic day for freedom  after a long road the supreme court  recognizes the right to the recreational  use of marijuana  but although lighting up is now not  illegal it's not exactly legal either  there is still a gray area because there  is no legislation the supreme court does  not legislate they are only crossing out  unconstitutional  articles in other words the supreme  court can only decriminalize pot  they can't come up with laws to regulate  its use that's a job for the senate and  congress  and one they've been trying to do for  the last couple of years shuttling  legislation back and forth between them  that would set rules for consuming  growing and selling  but they've never quite got it approved  the supreme court's decision will push  them to get on with that  now it's up to mexico's politicians both  here in the senate  and also in the house of congress to  finally get the legislation done  to regulate this new market and make  sure that it's a responsible one  rather than dissolving into chaos  at the moment the supreme court decision  only allows marijuana use  and with a permit a whole lot more needs  to be decided  how would a market to sell pot work how  to balance corporations and mexican  farmers  the politicians are still pondering  those questions  but this is a step forward john holman  al jazeera  mexico city
 

